    "answer": {
      "short": "No. A counterexample is $a_n:0,1,0,1,\\ldots$ and $b_n:0,0,1,1,\\ldots$.\n\nEach can be obtained from the other by order-preserving deletion, but their second terms differ.",
      "worked": "No. Consider the repeating sequences\n$$a_n:0,1,0,1,0,1,0,1,\\ldots$$\n$$b_n:0,0,1,1,0,0,1,1,\\ldots$$\nKeeping one term from each consecutive pair of equal terms in $b_n$ gives $a_n$.\n\nIn $a_n$, keep terms at positions $1,3,4,6,7,9,10,12,\\ldots$, repeating this selection every six positions. This gives $b_n$ without changing the order.\n\nThus each is a subsequence of the other, but $a_2=1$ and $b_2=0$, so they are different sequences.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
